id: pm-xgah3a
title: "A sandboxed fixture records provenance from the host harness environment, so the suite is green on CI and deterministically red for any agent running it locally"
description: "A sandboxed test fixture that simulates one harness records provenance derived from the environment of whatever harness is running the test process, so the suite's verdict is a function of the developer's machine rather than of the fixture.\n\nReproduced deterministically at commit 9a0a048b6, twice, once under a full coverage run and once in isolation. The failing test is tests/unit/sdk/agent-runtime-primitives.spec.ts, attributes update and close mutations to the detected harness. It constructs a simulated codex actor and asserts the exact shape of the two history entries that result. On this machine both entries carry an extra context block, agent_provenance_outcomes.version, with status failed, reason resolver_failed, resolver ai_agent_version and rule_version v1. The assertion compares whole entries, so the unexpected block fails it. With the single environment variable AI_AGENT removed the same spec passes sixteen of sixteen. That is the whole difference.\n\nThe mechanism is a three-step leak and every step is ordinary. The codex harness descriptor at src/core/shared/author.ts:219 declares provenance_resolvers with version mapped to ai_agent_version. The resolver at src/core/shared/author.ts:569 reads env.AI_AGENT and matches a version anchored at the end of the string. The outcome classifier at src/core/shared/author.ts:897 decides between failed and unavailable by asking only whether env.AI_AGENT is non-blank, not whether the value belongs to the harness the descriptor matched.\n\nSo on a hosted runner AI_AGENT is unset, the classifier reports unavailable with reason harness_unavailable, and the expectation holds. On the machine of an agent running the suite locally AI_AGENT is set by that agent's own harness. Here it is claude-code_2-1-226_agent, which ends in the literal text agent rather than a version, so the regex does not match, the resolver returns nothing, and because the variable was nevertheless present the classifier upgrades the outcome from unavailable to failed and stamps resolver_failed into the record. A simulated codex actor's immutable history entry therefore carries a diagnostic about the real Claude Code process that ran the test.\n\nThe first consequence is a local and hosted divergence with the wrong polarity, and it lands on precisely the audience this project exists to serve. Continuous integration is green at this commit. Any coding agent that runs the documented local command sees a deterministic red suite, and both harnesses this project targets set AI_AGENT. Local verification is meant to be at least as strict as hosted verification so that a green local run predicts a green push. Here it is differently strict rather than more strict: it fails on something hosted cannot observe while hosted passes on something local cannot reach. A contributor meeting this has no signal distinguishing it from a real regression, and the natural response, re-running or assuming a flake, is wrong in both directions.\n\nThe second consequence is the deeper one and is about the product rather than the suite. The sandbox does not isolate harness provenance. withTempPmPath redirects PM_PATH and PM_GLOBAL_PATH and neutralizes telemetry, so storage is contained, but the harness signal environment passes straight through. Any recorded provenance produced inside a sandbox is therefore a function of the host. For an outcome milestone whose claim is provable history, a field of the record that varies with who ran the process is a containment breach rather than a flaky assertion, and it is the same class of defeat that reproducible byte-identical workspace construction was built to rule out: two hosts constructing the same fixture do not produce the same record.\n\nThere is also a narrower correctness point worth separating, because it is true independently of any test. A resolver should only read an environment variable belonging to the harness whose descriptor matched. AI_AGENT is a shared name that several harnesses write, so the codex descriptor reading it means a codex actor can inherit a value written by a different tool entirely. The classifier compounds this by treating mere presence of the variable as evidence that the resolver had valid input, which is what turns an honest unavailable into a misleading failed. A record that says a resolver failed, when in fact it was handed another harness's value, states something untrue about the run."
type: Issue
